{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>כִּי צַיִד בְּפִיו</b> (\"for game was in his mouth\"). The commentators (Ibn Ezra and Radak) said: it means \"for he would put game in his mouth,\" that is, in Isaac's mouth, or \"he would bring\" — and the verb is elided. So too \"לַמּוֹעֵד אֲשֶׁר שְׁמוּאֵל\" (1 Samuel 13:8) — \"at the appointed time that Samuel [had set]\"; and \"וַה' אוֹתִי כְּגִבּוֹר עָרִיץ\" (Jeremiah 20:11) — \"and YHWH [made] me like a mighty warrior,\" i.e., He set me or appointed me. And it is also possible to interpret: \"And Isaac loved Esav because in Isaac's mouth was game continually\" — all day long he would crave to eat game, and it was always in his mouth; he would eat nothing else, and Esav was the one who brought it to him, as it says, \"a man who knows hunting.\" And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it is a figure of speech: Esav is \"game in his father's mouth\" — one designates a man by his characteristic activity, by its constancy. Similar to it: \"שִׁבְתְּךָ בְּתוֹךְ מִרְמָה\" (Jeremiah 9:5) — \"your dwelling is in the midst of deceit\"; and likewise \"וְאַנִי תְּפִלָּה\" (Psalms 109:4) — \"but I am prayer.\" And so they said (Genesis Rabbah 63:10): \"The lad is as good as the food is good for his mouth\" — קֻפְרָא טָבָא לְפֻמֵּהּ כָּסָא טָבָא לְפֻמֵּהּ."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:56.872845553Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}